# Does the news cover what actually kills us?
subtitle: "Play newspaper editor and share out a hundred stories on causes of death, then watch the bars turn into what Americans actually died of."
tags: ["health","data","psychology"]
format: quiz
thumb: "band \"Read all about it\" note \"News vs reality\" card teal"
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | a warm, clear quiz host who loves a good fact; patient, natural pace","b":"gemini:Puck | a curious co-host guessing out loud; relaxed and good-humoured, never exaggerated","take":"lines"}

## Does the news cover what actually kills us?
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Heart
      - Cancer
      - Homicide
      - Terror
      - Other
    values:
      - [3, 4, 42, 18, 33]
      - [29, 27, 1, 0, 43]
    stage: 0
    value_labels: true
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 50
    y_label: Share (%)
    bar_colors: each
    label_size: 34
    icons:
      - {of: anatomical heart, set: twemoji}
      - {of: hospital, set: twemoji}
      - {of: police car light, set: twemoji}
      - {of: bomb, set: twemoji}
      - null
    box:
      x: 100
      'y': 200
      w: 700
      h: 400
sources:
    - id: owid_news
      title: Does the news reflect what we die from?
      authors: Hannah Ritchie, Tuna Acisu and Edouard Mathieu
      year: 2025
      url: https://ourworldindata.org/does-the-news-reflect-what-we-die-from
      finding: 'US, 2023, 15 causes of death: heart disease 29 % and cancer 27 % of deaths but 2.8 % and 4.1 % of New York Times coverage; homicide under 1 % of deaths but 42 % of coverage; terrorism, 16 deaths, 18 % of coverage — OWID puts its coverage at about 18,240 times its share of deaths (shares within the 15 causes); the Washington Post and Fox News look much the same'
    - id: cdc
      title: Deaths and mortality (FastStats)
      authors: US Centers for Disease Control and Prevention
      year: 2025
      finding: US deaths by cause, 2023 (the death shares in the OWID analysis)
    - id: mediacloud
      title: Media Cloud
      authors: Media Cloud
      year: 2025
      finding: counts of news articles mentioning each cause of death (the coverage shares in the OWID analysis)

    text tag_news "NYT stories" #2f6b8f cites ["owid_news"] x 250 y 580 font_size 52 hidden true
    text tag_deaths "US deaths" #b5482e cites ["owid_news"] x 250 y 580 font_size 52 hidden true
    path gh0 dashed #2f6b8f points [[-0.32,3],[0.32,3]] data true style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    path gh1 dashed #2f6b8f points [[0.6799999999999999,4],[1.32,4]] data true style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    path gh2 dashed #2f6b8f points [[1.68,42],[2.32,42]] data true style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    path gh3 dashed #2f6b8f points [[2.68,18],[3.32,18]] data true style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    path gh4 dashed #2f6b8f points [[3.68,33],[4.32,33]] data true style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    group ghosts members ["gh0","gh1","gh2","gh3","gh4"] hidden true
    text gh_l "- - - news" #2f6b8f x 600 y 615 font_size 36 hidden true

    card title "Does the news cover what kills us?"

A: Does the news cover what actually kills us? Take every story the New York Times ran in 2023 that mentions a cause of death.
    draw axes

B: Surely it does. Journalists cover what matters.
    draw tag_news

    ask "You are the editor: share out a hundred stories between these causes." on all budget 100 judge false account_label "Stories left" store ed
        right: Here is how the Times really split them: homicide alone got forty-two of every hundred.

B: Eighteen for terrorism? And heart disease and cancer get seven between them?
    highlight bar_4 bar_1 bar_2

    erase tag_news

A: Keep the news as dashes. Now the bars show what Americans actually died of that year.
    draw ghosts gh_l tag_deaths

A: Heart disease and cancer shoot up: more than half of all these deaths.
    animate stage 1 duration 3

B: And homicide, the paper's biggest bar, sinks to about one in a hundred.
    highlight bar_3

A: Terrorism killed sixteen people in the whole country. Its share of the news was about eighteen thousand times its share of the deaths.
    text t16 "16 deaths" #b5482e at.data [2.55,36] cites ["owid_news"] font_size 44
    text t18k "18,000×" #b5482e at.data [2.55,26] cites ["owid_news"] font_size 56

    erase t16 t18k

B: Fine, the Times is odd. Surely the other papers are different?
    highlight tag_deaths

A: The Washington Post and Fox News look much the same.
    text papers "Post, Fox: same" #5e584e at.data [2.55,28] cites ["owid_news"] font_size 42

    erase papers

A: News is what is new. A sudden death is a story; a slow one is a statistic. So when you wonder what to worry about, read the boring bars.
    highlight bar_1 bar_2

## Thumbnail
role: thumbnail

    icon paper size 300 set twemoji x 260 y 450 icon_look picture of newspaper hidden true
    text q1 "What makes" #2f2b26 x 690 y 530 font_size 80 hidden true
    text q2 "the news?" #b5482e x 690 y 420 font_size 80 hidden true
    thumb band "You be the editor" kind band hidden true

## Thumbnail
role: thumbnail

    icon heart size 260 set twemoji x 230 y 450 icon_look picture of "anatomical heart" hidden true
    icon bomb size 260 set twemoji x 770 y 450 icon_look picture of bomb hidden true
    text vs "vs" #8f887c x 500 y 460 font_size 110 hidden true
    thumb band "Which one sells papers?" kind band hidden true

## Thumbnail
role: thumbnail

    icon a size 160 set twemoji x 410 y 540 icon_look picture of "anatomical heart" hidden true
    icon b size 160 set twemoji x 600 y 540 icon_look picture of hospital hidden true
    icon c size 160 set twemoji x 410 y 320 icon_look picture of "police car light" hidden true
    icon e size 160 set twemoji x 600 y 320 icon_look picture of bomb hidden true
    thumb q "What would you print?" kind question hidden true
